1
00:00:03,750 --> 00:00:04,870
I don't know why I avoided

2
00:00:04,870 --> 00:00:06,270
the Harry Potter books for so long.

3
00:00:06,270 --> 00:00:07,550
These are great.

4
00:00:07,570 --> 00:00:09,170
I just started number six.

5
00:00:09,170 --> 00:00:10,720
That's a good one.

6
00:00:10,760 --> 00:00:12,660
Dumbledore dies in that one.

7
00:00:15,630 --> 00:00:18,150
Yeah, I know, I didn't see it coming, either.

8
00:00:19,230 --> 00:00:20,570
Why would you say that?

9
00:00:20,570 --> 00:00:21,830
You brought up the subject.

10
00:00:21,830 --> 00:00:24,290
I contributed an interesting fact on that subject.

11
00:00:24,290 --> 00:00:26,530
It's called the art of conversation.

12
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:28,190
Okay, your turn.

13
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:31,400
That was a huge spoiler.

14
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:33,130
Good.

15
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:35,710
What is wrong with you?

16
00:00:35,710 --> 00:00:37,980
If I did that, you'd bitch about it for weeks.

17
00:00:37,980 --> 00:00:39,160
Oh, really, Leonard?

18
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:41,270
Are you going to have another one of your hissy fits?

19
00:00:41,270 --> 00:00:43,160
Hissy fits? I have hissy fits?

20
00:00:43,160 --> 00:00:45,790
Yes, and I have a theory why.

21
00:00:45,790 --> 00:00:48,320
Because of your lactose intolerance,

22
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:50,040
you switched over to soy milk.

23
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:53,670
Soy contains estrogen-mimicking compounds.

24
00:00:53,670 --> 00:00:55,950
I think your morning Cocoa Puffs

25
00:00:55,950 --> 00:00:59,230
are turning you into a hysterical woman.

26
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:04,540
You are unbelievable.

27
00:01:04,540 --> 00:01:06,060
I don't know why I put up with you.

28
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:08,100
You're controlling, you're irritating...

29
00:01:08,100 --> 00:01:11,270
There you go again-- nag, nag, nag.

30
00:01:12,050 --> 00:01:14,400
You're only proving my point, little lady.

31
00:01:15,180 --> 00:01:17,310
You know what? Screw you, Sheldon.

32
00:01:17,310 --> 00:01:19,770
You are the most annoying person I have ever met.

33
00:01:19,770 --> 00:01:21,170
What? I'm annoying?

34
00:01:21,180 --> 00:01:23,440
You criticize my behavior all the time.

35
00:01:23,450 --> 00:01:27,530
"Sheldon, don't talk about your bowel movements over breakfast."

36
00:01:28,390 --> 00:01:30,460
"Sheldon, when the president of the university

37
00:01:30,460 --> 00:01:32,010
is giving a eulogy at a funeral,

38
00:01:32,010 --> 00:01:34,550
don't yawn and point at your watch."

39
00:01:34,550 --> 00:01:36,510
"Sheldon, don't throw away my shirts

40
00:01:36,510 --> 00:01:37,680
'cause you think they're ugly."

41
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:39,180
You're impossible.

42
00:01:39,970 --> 00:01:40,910
That's it. I don't...

43
00:01:40,910 --> 00:01:42,100
I don't have to put up with this.

44
00:01:42,100 --> 00:01:44,550
Actually, I have your signature on a roommate agreement

45
00:01:44,550 --> 00:01:46,080
that says you do.

46
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:50,170
Here's what I think of your roommate agreement.

47
00:01:53,690 --> 00:01:55,730
- You pick that up right now. - No.

48
00:01:55,730 --> 00:01:58,180
Roommate agreement, section 27, paragraph 5:

49
00:01:58,180 --> 00:02:00,170
The roommate agreement, like the American flag,

50
00:02:00,170 --> 00:02:02,210
cannot touch the ground.

51
00:02:03,170 --> 00:02:04,970
I don't care.

52
00:02:04,970 --> 00:02:07,180
I don't have to do anything you say because...

53
00:02:08,020 --> 00:02:10,340
I don't think I want to live here anymore.

54
00:02:11,180 --> 00:02:12,540
- Where are you going? - To live with Penny

55
00:02:12,540 --> 00:02:14,990
and not you, you crazy bastard.

56
00:02:14,990 --> 00:02:17,130
- Crazy bastard? - Yes.

57
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:20,040
- Leonard, wait. - What?

58
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:23,180
Dobby the elf dies in book seven.

59
00:02:54,710 --> 00:02:55,770
Here you go.

60
00:02:55,770 --> 00:02:57,520
Great. Come on in.

61
00:02:57,530 --> 00:02:58,840
What, you don't say thank you?

62
00:02:59,220 --> 00:03:00,240
It's my suitcase.

63
00:03:00,240 --> 00:03:02,510
I lent it to you two years ago.

64
00:03:02,650 --> 00:03:03,720
Well, then, I should tell you

65
00:03:03,720 --> 00:03:05,960
I broke the wheel and the handle.

66
00:03:09,070 --> 00:03:11,400
So, is everyone from Bernadette's company

67
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:12,340
going to Vegas?

68
00:03:12,340 --> 00:03:15,440
No, just me, her and a couple of the big wigs.

69
00:03:15,440 --> 00:03:17,550
- It's part of a bonus she got. - Cool.

70
00:03:17,550 --> 00:03:19,580
Did she discover a cure for something?

71
00:03:19,580 --> 00:03:20,670
Not exactly.

72
00:03:20,670 --> 00:03:23,570
They spent a ton of money developing this dandruff medication

73
00:03:23,570 --> 00:03:26,920
that had the side effect of horrible anal leakage.

74
00:03:29,430 --> 00:03:31,770
Is there a good anal leakage?

75
00:03:34,580 --> 00:03:35,820
Anyway, it was Bernie's idea

76
00:03:35,820 --> 00:03:38,750
to rebrand the stuff as a cure for constipation.

77
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:41,410
Way to make lemonade.

78
00:03:41,410 --> 00:03:44,740
You know, from around the corner where fudge is made.

79
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:50,570
- Hey, I got a favor to ask. - Sure.

80
00:03:50,830 --> 00:03:53,740
My mom's been kind of an emotional wreck

81
00:03:53,740 --> 00:03:56,570
since that dentist she was dating dumped her.

82
00:03:56,570 --> 00:03:59,050
Dumped her? What, did he use a forklift?

83
00:04:02,340 --> 00:04:03,810
Sorry.

84
00:04:03,810 --> 00:04:06,760
There's nothing funny about morbid obesity.

85
00:04:07,310 --> 00:04:08,890
She's huge. It was funny.

86
00:04:10,070 --> 00:04:11,820
Anyway, I was just hoping

87
00:04:11,820 --> 00:04:13,910
that maybe you could check in on her tomorrow night

88
00:04:13,910 --> 00:04:15,560
and make sure she's doing okay.

89
00:04:15,560 --> 00:04:17,980
Dude, I'm a single man.

90
00:04:17,980 --> 00:04:20,350
Saturday night is my party night.

91
00:04:21,490 --> 00:04:23,810
Really? What do you got going on?

92
00:04:23,810 --> 00:04:25,870
I don't know, maybe drive down to Hollywood,

93
00:04:25,870 --> 00:04:28,590
hit a few hot spots, see if I can get lucky.

94
00:04:28,590 --> 00:04:30,990
Okay, tell me if this sounds familiar.

95
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:34,690
You pay $15 to park,

96
00:04:34,690 --> 00:04:36,450
you stand on the sidewalk for an hour

97
00:04:36,450 --> 00:04:37,940
until you break down and give the bouncer

98
00:04:37,940 --> 00:04:39,090
20 bucks to let you in.

99
00:04:39,090 --> 00:04:40,230
You push your way to the bar,

100
00:04:40,230 --> 00:04:42,710
where you drink an $18 cosmopolitan,

101
00:04:42,710 --> 00:04:45,030
then you stare at a pretty girl

102
00:04:45,030 --> 00:04:46,800
and imagine your perfect life together.

103
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:48,310
Your children, grandchildren.

104
00:04:48,310 --> 00:04:49,740
Meanwhile, she leaves with a guy who claims

105
00:04:49,740 --> 00:04:51,900
he wrote Beverly Hills Chihuahua.

106
00:04:53,140 --> 00:04:54,890
Then you give up on anyone ever loving you,

107
00:04:54,890 --> 00:04:56,320
go to Marie Callender's, buy a pie

108
00:04:56,320 --> 00:04:58,990
and eat it in your car in the parking lot.

109
00:05:01,860 --> 00:05:02,970
What time should I be at your mother's?

110
00:05:02,970 --> 00:05:04,530
I told her around 7:00.

111
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:09,270
I swear, that man is the most egotistical,

112
00:05:09,270 --> 00:05:11,860
insufferable human being I have ever met.

113
00:05:11,860 --> 00:05:14,380
Yeah, but you two make such a cute couple.

114
00:05:16,170 --> 00:05:19,170
Like Burt and Ernie.

115
00:05:19,170 --> 00:05:23,580
You guys even teach me stuff about words and numbers.

116
00:05:23,580 --> 00:05:25,090
Well, I've had it. I am done.

117
00:05:25,090 --> 00:05:27,380
I can't... I can't live with him for one more minute.

118
00:05:27,380 --> 00:05:29,210
Where are you going to go?

119
00:05:29,210 --> 00:05:31,250
I was thinking here with you.

120
00:05:36,310 --> 00:05:37,580
That a problem?

121
00:05:37,580 --> 00:05:38,950
No, not at all.

122
00:05:38,950 --> 00:05:40,620
No, it's-it's great.

123
00:05:40,620 --> 00:05:41,750
It's terrific. I...

124
00:05:41,750 --> 00:05:43,970
You know, I just can't help feel bad about Sheldon.

125
00:05:43,970 --> 00:05:44,820
I mean...

126
00:05:44,820 --> 00:05:46,900
how's he going to get by without you?

127
00:05:47,570 --> 00:05:49,180
Ernie.

128
00:05:51,030 --> 00:05:52,100
He's got Amy now.

129
00:05:52,100 --> 00:05:54,500
Yeah, he does, but it's not the same.

130
00:05:54,500 --> 00:05:55,790
Why?

131
00:05:58,830 --> 00:06:00,380
Well, um, all right.

132
00:06:00,380 --> 00:06:02,720
You-you know how in Harry Potter and the Half-Blood Prince,

133
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:04,520
Ron didn't abandon Harry just because Harry started

134
00:06:04,520 --> 00:06:05,800
dating Ron's sister?

135
00:06:05,800 --> 00:06:08,500
Harry and Ginny get together?

136
00:06:12,990 --> 00:06:14,950
Sorry. Spoiler alert.

137
00:06:15,570 --> 00:06:17,660
My point is, as much as I want to live with you,

138
00:06:17,660 --> 00:06:20,360
I can't do it knowing how much Sheldon needs you.

139
00:06:20,360 --> 00:06:22,200
Please, the only thing he needs me for

140
00:06:22,200 --> 00:06:23,770
is to be his whipping boy,

141
00:06:23,770 --> 00:06:26,070
his-his stooge, his doormat.

142
00:06:26,070 --> 00:06:28,420
Well, you know what they say: if it ain't broke...

143
00:06:32,040 --> 00:06:34,370
It sounds to me like you don't want us to live together.

144
00:06:34,370 --> 00:06:36,160
No. No, no, I do.

145
00:06:36,160 --> 00:06:37,770
I do. It's just...

146
00:06:37,770 --> 00:06:39,900
I mean, it's a really big step.

147
00:06:39,900 --> 00:06:40,900
Is it?

148
00:06:40,900 --> 00:06:42,070
We're together all the time.

149
00:06:42,070 --> 00:06:43,660
Financially, it makes great sense.

150
00:06:43,660 --> 00:06:46,780
Can you think of one reason why we shouldn't do this?

151
00:06:47,210 --> 00:06:49,880
Well... um...

152
00:06:50,400 --> 00:06:53,000
- I'm just a little thirsty. - Yeah.

153
00:06:57,720 --> 00:06:59,030
I got nothing.

154
00:07:00,140 --> 00:07:01,690
Great. I'll go get my stuff.

155
00:07:02,840 --> 00:07:03,420
Good.

156
00:07:07,710 --> 00:07:09,220
Okay, all right, don't freak out.

157
00:07:09,220 --> 00:07:10,950
You can make this work.

158
00:07:10,950 --> 00:07:13,530
Oh, could you clear off a shelf for me in the bathroom?

159
00:07:13,530 --> 00:07:15,670
I take a lot of medicine.

160
00:07:17,460 --> 00:07:20,060
Oh, please let some of it be Xanax.

161
00:07:24,380 --> 00:07:26,400
Are you ready for dessert?

162
00:07:28,630 --> 00:07:30,390
No, thank you, Mrs. Wolowitz.

163
00:07:30,390 --> 00:07:32,870
As it is, I'm going to have to carry my stomach out of here

164
00:07:32,870 --> 00:07:35,780
like I'm a fireman rescuing an infant.

165
00:07:37,280 --> 00:07:38,180
Oh, please.

166
00:07:38,180 --> 00:07:41,270
You're a tall glass of brown water.

167
00:07:42,200 --> 00:07:43,570
Have dessert.

168
00:07:43,570 --> 00:07:45,200
Well, I-I really couldn't.

169
00:07:45,200 --> 00:07:47,690
But, uh, I've had a lovely time eating your brisket

170
00:07:47,690 --> 00:07:49,880
and hearing about the things you've had removed

171
00:07:49,880 --> 00:07:52,310
from your body over the years.

172
00:07:53,040 --> 00:07:55,970
Didn't know you could have a cyst inside another cyst.

173
00:07:56,670 --> 00:07:59,780
The doctor said they were like Russian nesting dolls.

174
00:08:01,370 --> 00:08:02,820
Well, if you have to go,

175
00:08:02,820 --> 00:08:06,080
how about I put a little doggie bag together for you?

176
00:08:06,080 --> 00:08:08,420
That would be lovely. Thank you.

177
00:08:12,430 --> 00:08:14,080
Mrs. Wolowitz, uh...

178
00:08:14,580 --> 00:08:16,460
are you okay?

179
00:08:16,460 --> 00:08:17,920
Don't mind me.

180
00:08:17,920 --> 00:08:19,390
I just cry when I'm lonely

181
00:08:19,390 --> 00:08:22,000
and have nothing to live for.

182
00:08:27,260 --> 00:08:28,780
I, uh...

183
00:08:28,780 --> 00:08:32,260
- I suppose I could stay for some dessert. - Great.

184
00:08:32,260 --> 00:08:34,600
- You like chocolate chip cheesecake? - Sure.

185
00:08:34,920 --> 00:08:36,770
I'll make one.

186
00:08:39,980 --> 00:08:43,800
One vintage Mr. Mxyzptlk action figure.

187
00:08:43,800 --> 00:08:45,290
That's Leonard's.

188
00:08:45,290 --> 00:08:47,650
Children's toy.

189
00:08:48,230 --> 00:08:51,160
One Star Trek: The Next Genxtion phaser.

190
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:52,670
That's Leonard's.

191
00:08:53,200 --> 00:08:55,130
Children's toy.

192
00:08:55,680 --> 00:08:59,610
One Game of Thrones collector's edition Longclaw sword.

193
00:08:59,610 --> 00:09:02,560
Oh... Leonard and I bought that together.

194
00:09:02,560 --> 00:09:06,020
That's a bit of an ethical conundrum.

195
00:09:06,600 --> 00:09:09,230
Eh, I'll keep it.

196
00:09:09,570 --> 00:09:11,720
So, uh, what's your plan moving forward?

197
00:09:11,720 --> 00:09:15,320
Uh, suppose I'll have to find and cultivate a new roommate.

198
00:09:15,320 --> 00:09:17,240
What a task that will be.

199
00:09:17,240 --> 00:09:20,830
Do you know how uncivilized Leonard was when I took him in?

200
00:09:21,800 --> 00:09:22,620
No.

201
00:09:22,620 --> 00:09:26,220
Oh, it took me forever to get him on a bathroom schedule.

202
00:09:26,850 --> 00:09:30,310
He would just go whenever the mood struck him.

203
00:09:30,940 --> 00:09:33,900
- Like a dog-boy. - Exactly.

204
00:09:34,270 --> 00:09:35,970
What if you could find a roommate

205
00:09:35,970 --> 00:09:37,460
who was a scientist

206
00:09:37,460 --> 00:09:40,680
and already familiar and comfortable with your ways?

207
00:09:40,680 --> 00:09:42,160
That would be ideal.

208
00:09:42,160 --> 00:09:43,950
If a person like that existed,

209
00:09:43,950 --> 00:09:47,090
I would sign on, no further questions asked.

210
00:09:47,600 --> 00:09:50,010
Great. Here I am!

211
00:09:54,930 --> 00:09:56,610
Wait.

212
00:09:56,610 --> 00:09:58,600
Here who is where?

213
00:10:00,140 --> 00:10:01,130
Me.

214
00:10:01,130 --> 00:10:03,720
Aren't I your perfect roommate?

215
00:10:05,250 --> 00:10:06,750
Think about it, Sheldon.

216
00:10:06,750 --> 00:10:08,000
I'm not a stranger,

217
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:09,440
we're intellectually compatible,

218
00:10:09,440 --> 00:10:11,610
I'm willing to chauffeur you around town,

219
00:10:11,610 --> 00:10:13,020
and your personality quirks,

220
00:10:13,020 --> 00:10:15,250
which others find abhorrent or rage-inducing,

221
00:10:15,250 --> 00:10:17,140
I find cute as a button.

222
00:10:18,550 --> 00:10:20,180
What do you think?

223
00:10:27,650 --> 00:10:30,930
Tell me one reason why this isn't a fantastic idea.

224
00:10:33,630 --> 00:10:35,570
See? You can't.

225
00:10:35,690 --> 00:10:37,150
I'm gonna go see if Leonard's room

226
00:10:37,150 --> 00:10:39,570
is big enough for my water bed.

227
00:10:45,460 --> 00:10:48,800
Good buddy Leonard. Good buddy Leonard.

228
00:10:48,800 --> 00:10:50,730
Good buddy Leonard.

229
00:10:53,290 --> 00:10:54,230
What do you want?

230
00:10:54,230 --> 00:10:56,220
Hey, good buddy.

231
00:10:56,850 --> 00:10:59,300
So, uh, I was just talking to Amy,

232
00:10:59,300 --> 00:11:00,960
and she made me realize

233
00:11:00,960 --> 00:11:03,110
that this little dust-up between you and me

234
00:11:03,110 --> 00:11:05,090
is much ado about nothing.

235
00:11:05,690 --> 00:11:06,810
- Is that so? - Yes.

236
00:11:06,810 --> 00:11:09,000
All is forgiven, so come back home.

237
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,490
I'll make you some soy hot chocolate,

238
00:11:11,490 --> 00:11:13,740
and then I'll you tell you about our exciting new

239
00:11:13,740 --> 00:11:16,260
devil-may-care bathroom policy.

240
00:11:20,080 --> 00:11:22,350
- Cut to the chase, Sheldon. - Okay.

241
00:11:22,350 --> 00:11:24,360
Amy's decided she wants to move in with me,

242
00:11:24,420 --> 00:11:27,450
so I need you to come back home, you lovable scamp.

243
00:11:29,100 --> 00:11:30,810
That's a lot of product.

244
00:11:32,410 --> 00:11:33,690
- What's going on? - Oh, get this:

245
00:11:33,690 --> 00:11:35,490
suddenly, Sheldon wants me back

246
00:11:35,490 --> 00:11:37,230
because Amy wants to move in with him.

247
00:11:37,230 --> 00:11:39,450
Really? Interesting.

248
00:11:40,210 --> 00:11:42,360
Well, too late, pal. I'm not going anywhere.

249
00:11:42,360 --> 00:11:44,430
Penny and I are very happy living together.

250
00:11:44,430 --> 00:11:45,820
Isn't that right?

251
00:11:45,820 --> 00:11:48,900
It's like the happiness won't ever leave the apartment.

252
00:11:49,610 --> 00:11:50,810
Leonard, please.

253
00:11:50,810 --> 00:11:53,290
You know Amy moving in marks a level of intimacy

254
00:11:53,290 --> 00:11:54,920
our relationship isn't ready for.

255
00:11:54,920 --> 00:11:57,120
Yes! That is a real thing.

256
00:11:57,870 --> 00:11:59,740
And it doesn't mean you don't care about each other;

257
00:11:59,770 --> 00:12:01,180
it just means things are moving at a pace

258
00:12:01,180 --> 00:12:03,520
you're not comfortable with, and that's fine.

259
00:12:04,130 --> 00:12:05,450
Well, if he doesn't want to live with her,

260
00:12:05,450 --> 00:12:07,510
then he should tell her how he feels.

261
00:12:07,510 --> 00:12:08,690
Well, maybe he doesn't know how to say it

262
00:12:08,690 --> 00:12:10,310
without hurting her feelings.

263
00:12:10,310 --> 00:12:11,180
Feelings?

264
00:12:11,180 --> 00:12:13,430
What am I, a hippie at a love-in? No.

265
00:12:14,170 --> 00:12:15,060
The problem is,

266
00:12:15,060 --> 00:12:17,030
she laid out a series of logical arguments

267
00:12:17,030 --> 00:12:18,100
that I couldn't refute.

268
00:12:18,100 --> 00:12:20,400
- That is the worst, isn't it? - Yes.

269
00:12:21,050 --> 00:12:23,530
I'm sorry, Sheldon. I can't help you.

270
00:12:23,530 --> 00:12:24,580
Oh, spoiler alert:

271
00:12:24,580 --> 00:12:26,890
this door's about to slam in your face.

272
00:12:28,240 --> 00:12:29,760
Oh, there you are.

273
00:12:29,760 --> 00:12:33,010
When do I get a key to our apartment?

274
00:12:50,890 --> 00:12:52,040
Hello?

275
00:12:52,040 --> 00:12:54,450
Hey. How'd it go last night with my mom?

276
00:12:54,450 --> 00:12:56,000
Okay, I guess.

277
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:57,430
What time did you leave?

278
00:12:59,650 --> 00:13:02,150
Actually, I'm still here.

279
00:13:03,440 --> 00:13:05,460
What? You spent the night?

280
00:13:05,460 --> 00:13:06,420
Yeah.

281
00:13:07,470 --> 00:13:11,250
After dinner, we watched a rerun of Rockford Files,

282
00:13:11,250 --> 00:13:12,830
and then she opened a bottle

283
00:13:12,830 --> 00:13:14,990
of, uh, cream sherry, and the next thing I know,

284
00:13:14,990 --> 00:13:17,840
she was tucking me into your bed.

285
00:13:19,660 --> 00:13:21,610
You wore my pajamas?

286
00:13:23,150 --> 00:13:24,630
How do you sleep in these things?

287
00:13:24,630 --> 00:13:26,470
Silk pajamas on satin sheets?

288
00:13:26,470 --> 00:13:29,680
I slid out of the bed, like, three times.

289
00:13:30,950 --> 00:13:31,910
Rajesh!

290
00:13:31,910 --> 00:13:32,790
You up?

291
00:13:32,790 --> 00:13:34,300
You ready for breakfast?

292
00:13:34,300 --> 00:13:36,410
Oh, boy. Breakfast.

293
00:13:37,390 --> 00:13:38,750
Okay, Raj, listen to me.

294
00:13:38,750 --> 00:13:39,870
You need to get out of there.

295
00:13:39,870 --> 00:13:41,570
But I have a cream sherry hangover

296
00:13:41,570 --> 00:13:43,980
and I smell blintzes.

297
00:13:45,520 --> 00:13:46,240
If you don't leave now,

298
00:13:46,240 --> 00:13:47,810
she'll use food and guilt

299
00:13:47,810 --> 00:13:49,960
to keep you there for the rest of your life.

300
00:13:49,960 --> 00:13:51,150
Oh, Howard, stop.

301
00:13:51,150 --> 00:13:52,380
Trust me, you're not Jewish.

302
00:13:52,380 --> 00:13:54,060
That's how they get you.

303
00:13:55,150 --> 00:13:56,340
You're being silly.

304
00:13:56,340 --> 00:13:58,290
I can leave whenever I want.

305
00:13:58,290 --> 00:14:00,900
Oh, really? Where are your clothes and your shoes?

306
00:14:00,900 --> 00:14:03,320
They're on the chair, right over...

307
00:14:04,810 --> 00:14:06,760
Oy vey.

308
00:14:12,560 --> 00:14:14,350
Hello, home wrecker.

309
00:14:16,770 --> 00:14:18,100
What did I do?

310
00:14:18,100 --> 00:14:20,310
You gave Leonard somewhere to go.

311
00:14:20,310 --> 00:14:20,810
Thanks to you,

312
00:14:20,820 --> 00:14:23,750
Amy's out buying his and hers bath towels.

313
00:14:23,750 --> 00:14:25,370
Like I'd ever dry myself with something

314
00:14:25,370 --> 00:14:27,710
that has a possessive pronoun on it.

315
00:14:28,940 --> 00:14:30,660
Okay, listen.

316
00:14:31,430 --> 00:14:33,820
The truth is I don't want him living with me.

317
00:14:33,870 --> 00:14:35,460
Great. Kick him out.

318
00:14:35,460 --> 00:14:37,370
Break his heart. Everybody wins.

319
00:14:38,020 --> 00:14:39,240
No, I don't want to break his heart.

320
00:14:39,240 --> 00:14:40,150
I love him.

321
00:14:40,150 --> 00:14:41,700
This is just happening too fast.

322
00:14:41,700 --> 00:14:43,330
You think this is fast?

323
00:14:43,330 --> 00:14:44,140
It's just a matter of time

324
00:14:44,140 --> 00:14:47,120
before I see Amy's leg stubble in my shower.

325
00:14:47,720 --> 00:14:48,770
Yeah, and I've seen those legs.

326
00:14:48,770 --> 00:14:50,890
You might want to get some Drano.

327
00:14:51,940 --> 00:14:53,380
Hold on.

328
00:14:53,380 --> 00:14:55,130
If you don't want to live with Leonard,

329
00:14:55,130 --> 00:14:56,360
why don't you just tell him?

330
00:14:56,360 --> 00:14:57,660
Well, you know how he is.

331
00:14:57,660 --> 00:14:59,250
He's sensitive and emotional.

332
00:14:59,250 --> 00:15:01,680
That's because he drinks too much soy milk.

333
00:15:03,570 --> 00:15:04,660
Well, I don't know what else we can do

334
00:15:04,660 --> 00:15:06,450
but tell them the truth.

335
00:15:06,450 --> 00:15:07,950
I suppose there's no choice

336
00:15:07,950 --> 00:15:10,780
but to face the crying, angry accusations

337
00:15:10,780 --> 00:15:13,320
and the high-pitched wails of despair.

338
00:15:13,750 --> 00:15:14,360
Yeah.

339
00:15:14,360 --> 00:15:16,730
And who knows how Amy will react.

340
00:15:20,980 --> 00:15:22,530
Here's some more ice.

341
00:15:22,530 --> 00:15:23,960
Oh, thanks.

342
00:15:23,960 --> 00:15:25,640
What were we thinking?

343
00:15:25,640 --> 00:15:28,690
We should have just done it the regular way.

344
00:15:29,760 --> 00:15:31,670
Those Chinese acrobats in Cirque du Soleil

345
00:15:31,670 --> 00:15:33,770
made it look so easy.

346
00:15:35,080 --> 00:15:37,000
Honestly, if I could bend that far,

347
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,290
what would I need with you?

348
00:15:41,500 --> 00:15:44,730
If you could bend that far, you'd be doing us both a favor.

349
00:15:52,530 --> 00:15:54,120
Hey, Raj. What's up?

350
00:15:54,120 --> 00:15:55,240
You were right!

351
00:15:55,240 --> 00:15:56,470
I can't get out of here!

352
00:15:57,760 --> 00:15:59,610
You're still at my mother's?

353
00:15:59,610 --> 00:16:00,610
I'm trapped.

354
00:16:00,610 --> 00:16:02,320
My clothes have been in the laundry all day,

355
00:16:02,320 --> 00:16:03,990
and she hid my keys.

356
00:16:03,990 --> 00:16:05,400
I think they might be in her bra,

357
00:16:05,400 --> 00:16:08,110
because she jingles when she walks.

358
00:16:08,110 --> 00:16:10,040
What do I do?

359
00:16:10,040 --> 00:16:11,450
Hey, you wanted a woman in your life.

360
00:16:11,450 --> 00:16:12,830
Now you got one.

361
00:16:13,790 --> 00:16:15,720
Come on, Howard, help me.

362
00:16:15,720 --> 00:16:19,770
Rajesh, tatellah, I ran you a bath.

363
00:16:19,770 --> 00:16:21,510
Oh, my God.

364
00:16:21,510 --> 00:16:24,560
She's not gonna bathe me, is she?

365
00:16:27,310 --> 00:16:30,380
Gee, I wish I could tell you no.

366
00:16:34,500 --> 00:16:36,750
All right, well, thanks again for helping me out.

367
00:16:36,750 --> 00:16:38,270
But, Howard...

368
00:16:38,910 --> 00:16:40,800
Should we go back and rescue him?

369
00:16:40,800 --> 00:16:41,540
It's too late.

370
00:16:41,540 --> 00:16:43,680
We'll see him at his Bar Mitzvah.

371
00:16:46,190 --> 00:16:47,620
Here you go.

372
00:16:47,620 --> 00:16:50,580
I picked up the Chinese food just the way Leonard used to.

373
00:16:50,580 --> 00:16:52,360
- Is it kung pao chicken? - Yes.

374
00:16:52,360 --> 00:16:54,190
- Brown rice, not white rice? - Yes.

375
00:16:54,190 --> 00:16:56,570
- Spicy mustard from the Korean deli? - Yes.

376
00:16:56,570 --> 00:16:58,720
- I did good, right? - Yes.

377
00:17:01,740 --> 00:17:02,820
Amy,

378
00:17:02,820 --> 00:17:05,480
are you worried that us living together

379
00:17:05,480 --> 00:17:08,310
will take the mystery out of our relationship?

380
00:17:08,310 --> 00:17:09,050
No.

381
00:17:09,050 --> 00:17:10,840
Yeah, why would you?

382
00:17:11,450 --> 00:17:13,210
Oh, and, uh, check this out.

383
00:17:13,210 --> 00:17:14,630
I took the liberty of scripting

384
00:17:14,630 --> 00:17:17,470
a new outgoing voice mail message for both of us.

385
00:17:21,630 --> 00:17:23,330
"Hello. This is Sheldon."

386
00:17:23,330 --> 00:17:24,730
"And this is Amy."

387
00:17:24,730 --> 00:17:26,190
"We're not home right now."

388
00:17:26,190 --> 00:17:30,190
"'Cause we out dropping science, son."

389
00:17:34,990 --> 00:17:37,150
"Leave a message."

390
00:17:39,120 --> 00:17:40,560
Beep.

391
00:17:43,320 --> 00:17:45,290
You can't live here.

392
00:17:46,380 --> 00:17:48,190
What? Why?

393
00:17:48,700 --> 00:17:49,610
Is it the message?

394
00:17:49,610 --> 00:17:52,050
I only used urban slang to sound tough

395
00:17:52,050 --> 00:17:54,500
- so people wouldn't break in. - No.

396
00:17:54,500 --> 00:17:56,030
It's not the message.

397
00:17:56,030 --> 00:17:57,470
Well, what is it, then?

398
00:17:57,470 --> 00:17:59,540
I did everything just the way you like it.

399
00:17:59,540 --> 00:18:02,140
- You did. - Then what the hell, Sheldon?!

400
00:18:04,810 --> 00:18:06,550
We have been going out for over two years,

401
00:18:06,550 --> 00:18:08,850
and I have been nothing but patient with you!

402
00:18:08,850 --> 00:18:11,570
I watch your dopey space movies.

403
00:18:11,570 --> 00:18:13,300
I signed your ridiculous contract.

404
00:18:13,300 --> 00:18:15,060
I even stopped wearing lip gloss 'cause you said

405
00:18:15,060 --> 00:18:17,540
it made my mouth look too slippery.

406
00:18:19,210 --> 00:18:20,520
I am the best girlfriend

407
00:18:20,520 --> 00:18:21,870
you're ever gonna have.

408
00:18:21,870 --> 00:18:24,850
You give me one good reason why I can't live here.

409
00:18:25,770 --> 00:18:27,780
It's Penny's fault.

410
00:18:28,620 --> 00:18:29,310
What?

411
00:18:29,310 --> 00:18:30,330
She doesn't want to live with Leonard,

412
00:18:30,330 --> 00:18:31,960
so he has to come live here again.

413
00:18:31,960 --> 00:18:33,990
She's the snake in our garden.

414
00:18:35,440 --> 00:18:38,850
She's the reason we can't be happy.

415
00:18:40,570 --> 00:18:41,160
Hey, Ames. Yeah.

416
00:18:41,160 --> 00:18:42,430
"Hey, Ames," Nothing.

417
00:18:42,430 --> 00:18:43,920
I was all set to move in with Sheldon,

418
00:18:43,920 --> 00:18:45,000
and now I hear I can't

419
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:47,890
- 'cause you don't want to live with Leonard. - What?

420
00:18:49,720 --> 00:18:51,820
Sheldon, what did you say?

421
00:18:51,820 --> 00:18:53,250
I said the truth.

422
00:18:53,250 --> 00:18:55,360
You don't want to live with Leonard, and you know it.

423
00:18:55,360 --> 00:18:56,920
Since when don't you want to live with me?

424
00:18:56,920 --> 00:18:57,840
Oh, don't get all huffy.

425
00:18:57,840 --> 00:18:59,420
You're the one who decided to move in

426
00:18:59,420 --> 00:19:00,800
without even asking me if I was ready.

427
00:19:00,810 --> 00:19:02,440
Yeah, I think we should talk about that.

428
00:19:03,950 --> 00:19:05,530
And since you love the truth so much,

429
00:19:05,530 --> 00:19:07,330
why don't you tell Amy you don't want to live with her

430
00:19:07,330 --> 00:19:09,000
instead of blaming it on me?

431
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:11,580
I thought we were talking about the other thing.

432
00:19:12,600 --> 00:19:13,980
You're a coward.

433
00:19:13,990 --> 00:19:17,330
Well, the evidence does support that.

434
00:19:19,160 --> 00:19:20,850
Come on, Amy, let's go drink wine

435
00:19:20,850 --> 00:19:23,410
and talk about what jerks our boyfriends are.

436
00:19:23,410 --> 00:19:24,710
You know what would show them?

437
00:19:24,710 --> 00:19:26,950
I should move in here with you.

438
00:19:42,760 --> 00:19:44,060
Do you want to...

439
00:19:44,070 --> 00:19:46,130
catch up on some Walking Dead?

440
00:19:47,090 --> 00:19:47,920
Okay.

441
00:19:49,800 --> 00:19:52,360
Have you seen the one where Lori dies?

442
00:19:56,940 --> 00:19:58,680
No.

443
00:20:00,220 --> 00:20:01,590
Or maybe she doesn't.

444
00:20:01,590 --> 00:20:03,390
Let's find out.

445
00:20:16,630 --> 00:20:18,560
- Where you going? - No!

